//===-- type_traits - the eight traits a dtype dispatch is written in ----===//
//
// `std::is_same_v`, `std::conditional_t`, and the six names they are built
// out of. This is what `#include <type_traits>` resolves to when the compiler
// is NVRTC and the include path is a header set carried in the binary.
//
// # Why this is not a guard, either
//
// For the same reason `cstdint` in this directory is not: the names are used
// by device code, so guarding the directive away with `#ifndef __CUDACC_RTC__`
// deletes the include and leaves the uses. Measured over FlashInfer 0.6.15's
// attention closure: `std::is_same_v` 40 times, `std::conditional_t` 18,
// `std::is_same` 8, and one `std::void_t` / `std::declval` pair inside
// `utils.cuh`'s `DEFINE_HAS_MEMBER`, which `decode.cuh` expands at namespace
// scope on its first line of code. Every one of them is a compile-time
// question asked by a `__device__` template about its own dtype parameter.
//
// NVRTC answers none of them: `namespace "std" has no member "is_same"` with
// an empty header set, and the same with the real CUDA device headers in it.
//
// # The list is the measurement, not the standard's surface
//
// A real `<type_traits>` is some seventy traits. This is eight, because eight
// is what the closure names, and the file must not grow into the other one:
// a second standard library is a second set of promises to disagree with the
// first. When a FlashInfer bump reaches for a ninth, the compile says so by
// name and the entry is one line -- which is a better failure than a
// half-right `is_convertible` nobody reads.
//
// `std::min` is deliberately absent, and its absence is the interesting one:
// `std::max` and `std::min` appear 58 times in FlashInfer's closure, and every
// one of those sites is inside a HOST dispatch function --
// `BatchDecodeWithPagedKVCacheDispatched` and its relatives -- which the
// vendored copies guard away wholesale under `__CUDACC_RTC__`, because under
// the JIT the launch is `cuLaunchKernel` from Rust and the host half of
// FlashInfer is exactly the half we replace. **That measurement stands and is
// why `min` is still not here.** `max` is below for a reason that has nothing
// to do with it and does not generalise to `min`; see its own entry.
//
// # §62.7: the three names CUTLASS asks for, added 2026-08-14
//
// Measured, not guessed. `nvrtcCompileProgram` on CUTLASS 3.x's grouped-GEMM
// stack at `compute_90a` produced SEVEN errors and they were three names:
//
// ```text
//   sm90_epilogue_array_tma_warpspecialized.hpp:497   std::is_pointer_v
//   sm100_epilogue_array_tma_warpspecialized.hpp:242  std::max
//   linear_combination_bias_elementwise.h:77          std::void_t
// ```
//
// A six-line prelude supplying those three closed all seven, and the concrete
// sm90 ptr-array grouped GEMM then compiled to 1,245,452 B of PTX with exactly
// one `.entry`. **CUTLASS needs a row in this file, not a patch set** -- which
// is a different order of cost from FA2's, and §13.6's price must not be
// quoted at it.
//
// `void_t` was already here (FlashInfer's `DEFINE_HAS_MEMBER` asks for it), so
// the addition is two names. All three sites are places where UPSTREAM wrote
// `std::` where the file's own convention is `cute::` or
// `cutlass::platform::`; from NVRTC's side they are indistinguishable from
// any other missing trait, and the eight-not-seventy rule this file states is
// unchanged -- the compile named them, and each entry is one line.

#ifndef PIE_NVRTC_TYPE_TRAITS_
#define PIE_NVRTC_TYPE_TRAITS_

namespace std {

/// The compile-time constant every trait below is a specialisation of.
template <class T, T v>
struct integral_constant {
  static constexpr T value = v;
  using value_type = T;
  using type = integral_constant;
  __host__ __device__ constexpr operator value_type() const noexcept { return value; }
  __host__ __device__ constexpr value_type operator()() const noexcept { return value; }
};

/// `integral_constant<bool, true>`, spelled as the standard spells it.
using true_type = integral_constant<bool, true>;
/// `integral_constant<bool, false>`.
using false_type = integral_constant<bool, false>;

/// Whether two types are the same type, ignoring nothing.
template <class T, class U>
struct is_same : false_type {};
template <class T>
struct is_same<T, T> : true_type {};
/// The variable form, which is how the closure spells it 40 times out of 48.
template <class T, class U>
inline constexpr bool is_same_v = is_same<T, U>::value;

/// `B ? T : F`, over types.
template <bool B, class T, class F>
struct conditional {
  using type = T;
};
template <class T, class F>
struct conditional<false, T, F> {
  using type = F;
};
/// The alias form, which is the only form the closure uses.
template <bool B, class T, class F>
using conditional_t = typename conditional<B, T, F>::type;

/// SFINAE: a `type` member only when `B`.
///
/// Asked for by carried upstream text, not by the closure:
///
/// ```text
///   tensorrt_llm/common/cudaUtils.h:393  ceilDiv's two integral guards
/// ```
///
/// which `csrc/src/moe/moe_glue.cuh` carries verbatim for its one device call
/// site. It is here rather than dropped from that copy for the charter's
/// reason: `ceilDiv` without the guard still compiles for `float` and
/// silently performs a float division, which is a wrong answer where the
/// whole point of a feature-subset shim is that a gap is a compile error.
template <bool B, class T = void>
struct enable_if {};
template <class T>
struct enable_if<true, T> {
  using type = T;
};
template <bool B, class T = void>
using enable_if_t = typename enable_if<B, T>::type;

/// The integral types, and only the ones this tree can present.
///
/// `enable_if_t`'s companion at the same site. Enumerated rather than
/// derived, because the standard's answer includes `char8_t`, `wchar_t`, the
/// `char16_t`/`char32_t` pair and every cv-qualified spelling of all of them,
/// and a partial list dressed as the standard trait is the kind of thing that
/// reads true and answers false. What is here is what a `ceilDiv` argument in
/// this tree has ever been, plus the sizes that would be a silent narrowing
/// if omitted. `bool` is included because the standard includes it; nothing
/// calls it that way.
template <class T>
struct is_integral : false_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<bool> : true_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<char> : true_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<signed char> : true_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<unsigned char> : true_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<short> : true_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<unsigned short> : true_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<int> : true_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<unsigned int> : true_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<long> : true_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<unsigned long> : true_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<long long> : true_type {};
template <> struct is_integral<unsigned long long> : true_type {};
template <class T>
inline constexpr bool is_integral_v = is_integral<T>::value;

/// SFINAE's discard: any type list, collapsed to `void`.
///
/// Two callers, one from each vendored library: FlashInfer's
/// `utils.cuh::DEFINE_HAS_MEMBER`, and CUTLASS's
/// `epilogue/thread/linear_combination_bias_elementwise.h:77`, which spells
/// `struct ElementwiseOpDispatcher<T, std::void_t<typename T::Arguments>>`.
template <class...>
using void_t = void;

/// Whether `T` is a pointer type.
///
/// One caller, and it is a `__CUTE_REQUIRES` constraint rather than a trait
/// used for dispatch: `cutlass/epilogue/collective/
/// sm90_epilogue_array_tma_warpspecialized.hpp:497` writes
/// `__CUTE_REQUIRES(std::is_pointer_v<TensorMapC>)` on the ptr-array
/// epilogue's tensormap parameter. Under NVRTC the failure is three errors
/// from one line -- the missing name, then *"type name is not allowed"* and
/// *"expected an expression"* as the constraint falls apart -- so the
/// diagnostic overstates the gap by a factor of three, which is worth knowing
/// before anyone counts errors as a measure of distance.
template <class T>
struct is_pointer : false_type {};
template <class T>
struct is_pointer<T*> : true_type {};
template <class T>
struct is_pointer<T* const> : true_type {};
template <class T>
struct is_pointer<T* volatile> : true_type {};
template <class T>
struct is_pointer<T* const volatile> : true_type {};
/// The variable form, which is the only form CUTLASS uses.
template <class T>
inline constexpr bool is_pointer_v = is_pointer<T>::value;

/// The larger of two values, `constexpr`.
///
/// **Read the note above about `std::min` before adding to this.** `min` is
/// absent on a measurement -- 58 sites, all in host dispatch functions that
/// the vendored FlashInfer guards away -- and that measurement is not what
/// puts `max` here. This one has a single caller and it is a compile-time
/// constant in a class body, not a host launcher:
///
/// ```text
/// cutlass/epilogue/collective/sm100_epilogue_array_tma_warpspecialized.hpp:242
///   constexpr static uint32_t NumTmaDescriptorsPerSm =
///       IsGroupedGemmKernel ? (NumMaxSchedulerPipelineStageCount
///           + std::max(StagesC, (ReuseSmemC ? StagesC : StagesD)) + 2) : 1;
/// ```
///
/// It is reached from the sm90 path too, because `collective_builder.hpp`
/// pulls every architecture's epilogue header and this is a class-scope
/// initialiser that must be parsed whether or not the specialisation is
/// selected. So it is not optional on an sm90-only carried set, and guarding
/// it away is not available: it is upstream text under a name we impersonate.
///
/// Declared for `const T&` and not by value because that is the overload the
/// standard names and the one a `constexpr static` initialiser binds to
/// without a copy. `min` still does not exist and a caller that wants it
/// should arrive with its own measurement.
template <class T>
__host__ __device__ constexpr const T& max(const T& a, const T& b) {
  return a < b ? b : a;
}

/// The smaller of two values, `constexpr`.
///
/// The caller the note above asked for, and it arrived with its own
/// measurement. Not a host dispatch function and not guarded away -- a
/// **device** compile-time constant, in the body of a `__global__`:
///
/// ```text
/// csrc/fused_moe/cutlass_backend/cutlass_fused_moe_kernels.cuh:1745
///   constexpr int64_t FINALIZE_ELEM_PER_THREAD =
///       128 / std::min(sizeof_bits<OutputType>::value, sizeof_bits<GemmOutputType>::value);
/// ```
///
/// `finalizeMoeRoutingKernel` and `finalizeMoeRoutingNoFillingKernel`, one
/// site each, and it sizes the `cutlass::Array` every thread of those kernels
/// loads and stores. So it is the same *shape* of fact that put `max` here --
/// a compile-time constant that must be evaluated, not a host call that can
/// be guarded -- reached from the other side of the host/device line.
///
/// The 58 host sites the note above measured are untouched by this: they are
/// still guarded away, and this declaration does not un-guard them. What
/// changed is that the measurement is no longer "58 sites, all host"; it is
/// "58 host sites, all guarded, plus two device sites that cannot be".
///
/// `const T&` for `max`'s reason, and because the two must keep the same
/// overload shape or a `std::min(a, b)` and a `std::max(a, b)` on the same
/// pair of expressions would bind differently.
template <class T>
__host__ __device__ constexpr const T& min(const T& a, const T& b) {
  return b < a ? b : a;
}

/// A value of `T` in an unevaluated context. Declared and never defined, as
/// the standard requires -- calling it is the diagnostic.
template <class T>
T&& declval() noexcept;

}  // namespace std

#endif  // PIE_NVRTC_TYPE_TRAITS_
